{
    "id": "q011",
    "question": "Why does the adversarial classifier struggle more against SHAP than against LIME, and how does this connect to the finding about OOD classifier accuracy?",
    "expected_answer": "SHAP is harder to fool because of its local accuracy property, which forces feature attributions to sum to the difference between a prediction and the average background prediction — this spreads importance across several features instead of letting it hide on just one, so the sensitive feature keeps surfacing (67-71% success with two uncorrelated features, versus 100% for LIME). This connects to the OOD classifier finding: SHAP starts responding to weaker OOD classifiers (F1 ~0.45) but needs a very accurate one to fully hide the bias, while LIME stays vulnerable to the bias feature until the OOD classifier crosses F1 ~0.8 and then becomes highly exploitable.",
    "source_doc": "fooling_shap.pdf",
    "source_page": [4, 5],
    "gold_chunk": [
        "This is due to SHAP\u2019s local accuracy property\nthat ensures that feature attributions must add up to the difference between a given prediction and the average prediction for\nthe background distribution",
        "SHAP, on the other hand, starts responding to the attacks with a\nless accurate OOD classifier (F1 score \u223c 0.45), however, the effect is\nmore gradual; it needs a very accurate OOD classifier to completely\nhide the bias"
    ],
    "type": "multi_hop"
}

{
    "id": "q012",
    "question": "How does the paper's PCA observation about perturbed data points relate to why the attack works at all?",
    "expected_answer": "The PCA visualization shows that perturbed instances (generated by LIME-style noise) are distributed very differently from real instances in the COMPAS data, even in a low-dimensional projection. This means it's easy to build a classifier (the OOD detector) that tells perturbed points apart from real ones. The attack exploits exactly this: it behaves like the biased classifier on real data, but switches to an unbiased-looking classifier on perturbed points, so LIME and SHAP -- which build their explanations mainly from perturbed points -- only ever see the innocuous behavior.",
    "source_doc": "fooling_shap.pdf",
    "source_page": 2,
    "gold_chunk": [
        "the synthetic data points generated\nfrom input perturbations are distributed significantly differently\nfrom the instances in the input data",
        "an adversary\ncan create an adversarial classifier (scaffolding) that behaves like\nthe original classifier"
    ],
    "type": "multi_hop"
}

