{
    "id": "q015",
    "question": "What is a common defense to the value-free ideal?",
    "expected_answer": "The common defense concedes part of the argument from inductive risk: it accepts that accepting or rejecting a hypothesis requires appealing to ethical values. But its response is that scientists should therefore never accept or reject hypotheses at all — they should only assign probabilities to hypotheses relative to a fixed set of evidence. Applied to COMPAS, this means the tool's role is only to output a confidence/risk score, not to make a determinate prediction. Under Northpointe's AIPIE model (Assessment, Interpretation, Plan, Implement, Evaluate), COMPAS operates only at the Assessment step; the actual predictive judgment — and the inductive risk that comes with it — falls to judges in the later interpretation, planning, and implementation steps, not to the tool itself.",
    "source_doc": "algorithms_value_free.pdf",
    "source_page": 23,
    "gold_chunk": "A popular defense of the value-free ideal in science begins by conceding part of the argument from inductive risk.",
    "type": "single_hop"
}

