{
    "id": "q016",
    "question": "How did the value-free ideal come about?",
    "expected_answer": "The value-free ideal originates from \"Dragnet objectivity\" — the folk view that scientists should judge hypotheses on \"just the facts,\" free of personal values (named after Sgt. Joe Friday's catchphrase from the 1950s-60s show Dragnet). The text argues this model is impossible: raw data alone always underdetermines which conclusion to draw (the problem of underdetermination of theory by evidence), which traces back to Hume's Problem of Induction — the fact that there's no justification for why past instances should support predictions about unknown instances. Since induction can't work on data alone, science (and machine learning) unavoidably requires non-evidential assumptions (\"canons of inductive inference,\" elsewhere called \"biases\") to narrow the hypothesis space. Hume's own answer was that assuming nature is uniform is simply a natural human instinct.",
    "source_doc": "algorithms_value_free.pdf",
    "source_page": [4, 5],
    "gold_chunk": "This is a form of objectivity that Antony (2001, 2006, 2016) calls",
    "type": "single_hop"
  }

