{
    "id": "q019",
    "question": "How does Rudner's seat-belt/pant-belt example connect to the machine learning case of the image recognition program a few paragraphs later?",
    "expected_answer": "Rudner's seat belt vs. pant belt buckle example shows that the same risk of a defective design matters far more in one context (seat belts, where failure is dangerous) than another (pant belts, trivial failure) — so the confidence threshold for accepting an engineering hypothesis must be set by ethical values, not fixed in advance. The paper then applies this directly to machine learning: an image-recognition program distinguishing human from non-human shapes needs a much higher accuracy threshold if it triggers a safety-relevant system than if it merely triggers office lights. Both examples make the same point — the acceptable level of inductive risk depends on the ethical stakes of getting it wrong, so setting that threshold is itself a value-laden decision, whether for a scientist's hypothesis or a machine learning model's classification.",
    "source_doc": "1785266102001_algorithms_value_free.docx",
    "source_page": [18, 19],
    "gold_chunk": [
      "Imagine that in one case, engineers are responsible\nfor producing seat belt buckles, while in another scenario, engineers are\nresponsible for producing pant belt buckles",
      "For example, imagine\nthat you are tasked with building an image recognition program to distinguish\nhuman shapes from non-human shapes. The level of inaccuracy you should\ntolerate will depend on the use to which your algorithm will be put."
    ],
    "type": "multi_hop"
  }

{
    "id": "q020",
    "question": "What is the relation between personal AIs and extending your mind?",
    "expected_answer": "The text frames personal AIs (like the GENIUS system from VERSES) as a form of extended mind: over time these systems learn a person's specific needs, interests, and choices, and eventually come to feel like \"borderline-you\" -- reliable, always-running, implicitly trusted components rather than external tools. Losing or deleting one would feel like a minor stroke, not like losing a gadget; the author says you'd still \"use\" such a system, but only in the same attenuated sense that you use your own hippocampus or frontal lobe. This connects to the extended-mind framing the author develops earlier: if you understand yourself as a bio-technologically distributed self rather than identifying your mind purely with your biological brain, then offloading cognitive work onto a personal AI isn't a loss of capacity but a redistribution of it -- \"the careful husbanding of our own on-board cognitive capital.\" On this view, personal AIs become part of \"the new hybrid you,\" and the real questions shift from \"am I losing brain function?\" to practical ones about reliability -- is the AI's storage or judgment fragile, corrupt, or untrustworthy for this particular task.",
    "source_doc": "clark_2025.pdf",
    "source_page": 3,
    "gold_chunk": [
      "You survive their\nloss or deletion, but much as you would a minor stroke.",
      "But what if you were already best understood as an extended\nmind, a bio-technologically distributed self?"
    ],
    "type": "multi_hop"
  }


